WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.280
How can a small graft make its host build a second spinal cord, from cells meant for

2
00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:09.280
skin?

3
00:00:09.583 --> 00:00:11.363
First, a little embryology.

4
00:00:12.243 --> 00:00:14.503
The egg divides into a ball of smaller cells.

5
00:00:15.567 --> 00:00:20.787
In amphibians, the ball becomes a blastula, a sphere of cells around a fluid-filled

6
00:00:20.787 --> 00:00:21.787
cavity.

7
00:00:22.267 --> 00:00:27.507
Then, in gastrulation, cells move inside, and the embryo sorts into three layers.

8
00:00:28.567 --> 00:00:32.687
The outer layer, the ectoderm, makes the outer skin and the nervous system.

9
00:00:33.650 --> 00:00:38.470
The middle layer, the mesoderm, makes muscle, bone and the notochord, a stiff rod

10
00:00:38.470 --> 00:00:41.410
along the back. The inner layer lines the gut.

11
00:00:44.783 --> 00:00:47.723
Cells move inside through a slit, the blastopore.

12
00:00:48.563 --> 00:00:50.843
Its edge on the future back is the dorsal lip.

13
00:00:51.783 --> 00:00:53.303
Now watch the ectoderm.

14
00:00:54.123 --> 00:00:55.123
It looks like one sheet.

15
00:00:56.083 --> 00:00:58.783
Along the back, a strip becomes the neural plate.

16
00:00:59.463 --> 00:01:03.263
It rolls up into the neural tube, the future brain and spinal cord.

17
00:01:04.283 --> 00:01:07.483
On the belly, the same sheet stays flat and becomes skin.

18
00:01:08.533 --> 00:01:12.453
So why does the back strip become neural, while the belly strip becomes skin?

19
00:01:18.917 --> 00:01:20.397
There are two kinds of answer.

20
00:01:21.457 --> 00:01:23.697
Perhaps the belly cells are already fixed as skin.

21
00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:29.130
Or they can still change, and something nearby tells the back strip what to become.

22
00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:32.450
Moving tissue tests the first answer.

23
00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:36.190
Put dorsal-lip tissue next to belly ectoderm, and watch.

24
00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:41.410
The graft worked. The belly cells built a second neural tube.

25
00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:44.130
Chapter two shows whose cells they were.

26
00:01:45.083 --> 00:01:47.943
That experiment gives this lesson its one idea.

27
00:01:48.967 --> 00:01:51.147
A cell's fate can be set by its neighbours.

28
00:01:52.267 --> 00:01:56.947
Move the organizer somewhere new, and host cells there build much of a second back

29
00:01:56.947 --> 00:02:00.007
around it. The graft itself becomes notochord.

30
00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:04.580
But only while they are still competent, that is, able to answer.

31
00:02:09.983 --> 00:02:14.163
Notice one limit. The idea says who changed fate, and where.

32
00:02:15.023 --> 00:02:16.103
Not what the signal is.

33
00:02:17.033 --> 00:02:19.993
One sheet, two fates, and neighbours can decide which.

34
00:02:20.873 --> 00:02:22.773
Next: whose cells built the second back?
